---
import race from '../content/race.json';

// **The morning, in order — one copy, because two pages need exactly these rows.**
//
// `/nn/2026/race-day/` shows it as the running order somebody reads on the morning.
// `/nn/2026/` shows it as what the day looks like before they decide to enter. It is the same
// six rows either way, and six rows written out twice is six rows that drift — which has
// already happened once here in a shape worth remembering: #121 found three hardcoded `10:30`s
// in `race-day.astro`'s prose that `race.json` could not correct, and by then 10:30 had become
// the *briefing* rather than the walk. A stale time that names a real thing ten minutes early
// is worse than no time at all, because it reads as right.
//
// So the array is the only source and this is the only renderer of it. `terms.astro` reads one
// row by `id` for a different purpose and states no time of its own.
//
// **The row `<div>` is deliberate and is valid inside a `<dl>`.** It is what lets a row be a
// grid — the time in its own column, wrapping to a stacked row on a narrow screen — without
// the two halves of a pair drifting apart in the markup.
//
// **A description list rather than a table**, because each row is one time and one thing with
// no second column of data to line up, and because it is what degrades best at 320px, where a
// two-column table either scrolls sideways or squeezes the words to one per line.
//
// Nothing here styles the times. `nn-theme.css` owns that, and the tabular figures it sets are
// what keep the column rigid — see the type rules at the head of that file.
---

<dl class="nn-schedule">
  {
    race.schedule.map(({ time, what }) => (
      <div class="nn-schedule-row">
        <dt>{time}</dt>
        <dd>{what}</dd>
      </div>
    ))
  }
</dl>
